--- DISORDER ---
Neurocognitive Disorder / Substance Use Disorder

--- CASE STUDY ---

Emily was a 19-year-old woman who reluctantly agreed to see a psychiatrist at the insistence of her parents. She denied any personal concern, stating: "Everything is going great in my life. I have plenty of friends, go out almost every night, and always have lots of fun." She blamed her parents for wanting the evaluation.

Her parents reported that Emily had become increasingly irritable, unproductive, and oppositional over the past four years. She drank to intoxication almost nightly, sometimes not returning home for an entire weekend, and had used small amounts of marijuana, alprazolam, cocaine, and prescription stimulants. She had withdrawn from her longtime friends, engaged with a group of “dropouts and malcontents,” and her grades dropped dramatically from As to Ds.

Neuropsychological testing revealed a dramatic decline in cognitive performance. On the SAGE aptitude test, her percentile scores fell from the upper 10% as a 13-year-old to the bottom 10% as an adult. On the DAT, she scored below the high school average in all measures, reflecting significant decline in reasoning, spelling, and perceptual skills.

EEG, brain CT, and conventional MRI were normal, but a FLAIR T2-weighted MRI revealed a lesion in the left frontal cortex, highly suggestive of a prior traumatic brain injury (TBI). Collateral history revealed a motor vehicle accident four years prior in which Emily hit her head on a dashboard, experienced posttraumatic amnesia and confusion, and had persistent headaches for weeks. No immediate medical attention had been sought.

Discussion: Emily’s sudden academic, behavioral, and personality changes were consistent with mild neurocognitive disorder due to TBI. Substance use may have been a maladaptive coping mechanism for cognitive and social difficulties. Although her cognitive deficits are significant, she remains independent in daily living activities. Her behavioral disturbances include impulsivity, oppositionality, and risky substance use.

--- BEHAVIOUR ---
- Reluctant to engage, deflects concern onto parents
- Defiant and oppositional
- Dismissive of personal issues
- Socially active but with high-risk peer group
- Resistant to insight
- Displays mild impulsivity and poor judgment

--- SYMPTOMS from DSM-5 ---
- Cognitive decline (reasoning, perceptual skills, executive function)
- Behavioral disturbance (oppositionality, impulsivity)
- Substance use (alcohol, cannabis, stimulants, benzodiazepines)
- Memory impairment (posttraumatic amnesia noted after accident)
- Personality change (irritability, rebelliousness, academic disengagement)

